        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "7ee9a278-b953-5a00-8e21-92c2a392fcf4",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Given the function $f(x,y)=x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2} + 3 x y$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(2,1)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Hold the other coordinate constant when taking each partial derivative. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2 x y + 2 y^{2} + 3 y \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 x \\\\ \\nabla f(2,1)&=\\langle 9,18\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(2,1)\\cdot\\langle x - 2,y - 1\\rangle&=0 \\\\ 9 x + 18 y - 36&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n\\langle 1,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 2,y - 1\\rangle=0\n$$",
          ":question/answer-fields": [
