          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent line at $P=(1,0)$ to the curve $2 x e^{x y} - 2 y^{2}=- x^{2} - 3 y + 3$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Move all terms to the left to define a level function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y)&=x^{2} + 2 x e^{x y} - 2 y^{2} + 3 y - 3 \\\\ F(1,0)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the chain rule to the exponential and the product rule to its coordinate multiplier. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F_x(x,y)&=2 x + \\left(2 x y + 2\\right) e^{x y} \\\\ F_y(x,y)&=2 x^{2} e^{x y} - 4 y + 3 \\\\ \\nabla F(1,0)&=\\langle 4,5\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla F(1,0)\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y\\rangle&=0 \\\\ 4 x + 5 y - 4&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n4 x + 5 y - 4=0\n$$",
